---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="workshop day — tiny soft computer"
  description="What attendees actually do during the tiny soft computer workshop."
  active="workshop"
>

  <section class="hero" style="padding-top: 2.5rem;">
    <p class="kicker">workshop day</p>
    <h1>your kit is ready. you're just here to make it yours.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      every kit arrives fully preloaded, CircuitPython, libraries, and
      code are already installed and tested. you won't be doing any of
      the technical setup, that part is already done. here's what you
      will do, in three parts.
    </p>
    <img
      class="step-image"
      src="/images/kit-overview.png"
      alt="Everything in a tiny soft computer kit: a Feather RP2040 ThinkInk board, a 24-pin flexible eInk display already showing tiny soft computer, two alligator clip wires, a USB-C cable, and two tactile buttons."
    >
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>what's in the kit</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>2.13" 212×104 Flexible Monochrome / 4-Gray eInk ePaper Display, ILI0373 chipset</li>
      <li>Adafruit RP2040 Feather ThinkInk for 24-pin E-Paper Displays, STEMMA QT</li>
      <li>2 soft silicone top 6mm push-buttons</li>
      <li>2 alligator clips</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div class="divider">✿</div>

  <section class="block">
    <span class="tag">part one</span>
    <h2>hardware: seat the display, wire the button</h2>
    <p>
      before touching any code, get the physical pieces connected. this
      comes first since the software part assumes the hardware already
      works.
    </p>

    <h3>seat the display</h3>
    <p>
      if your kit's display isn't already seated in the microcontroller's
      docking port, open the latches, insert the ribbon cable, and close
      the latches to hold it in place.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      go gently here. the ribbon cable is thin and the display is soft,
      it's not meant to take force. let it slide in on its own, don't
      push. remember, these are <em>soft</em> computers, treat the
      cable the way you'd want to be treated.
    </p>
    <img
      class="step-image"
      src="/images/hardware-step-0.png"
      alt="Step 0: using the latches on the microcontroller's docking port, insert the display's ribbon cable and close the latches to secure it."
    >
    <img
      class="step-image"
      src="/images/inserting-display-closeup.png"
      alt="A closer look at the display connector on the Feather board, showing where the ribbon cable seats into the docking port."
    >

    <h3>wire the button</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>find the button and its two wires in your kit.</li>
      <li>connect one wire to pin <code>A2</code> on the board.</li>
      <li>connect the other wire to any <code>GND</code> (ground) pin.</li>
      <li>that's it, no soldering and no resistors needed.</li>
    </ol>
    <img
      class="step-image"
      src="/images/hardware-step-1.png"
      alt="Step 1: wrap the wire at the end of the alligator clips around microcontroller pins labeled GND and A2. Make sure they're secure and not touching other pads."
    >
    <img
      class="step-image"
      src="/images/pins-closeup.png"
      alt="A labeled close-up of the A2 and GND pins on the Feather board, with wires already connected, for reference while wiring."
    >
    <p class="note">
      a button has 4 legs in 2 connected pairs. your two wires need to be
      on opposite sides. if the button doesn't respond, or responds
      backward, try moving one wire to a different leg.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="divider">✿</div>

  <section class="block">
    <span class="tag">part two</span>
    <h2>software: make the messages your own</h2>
    <p>
      the board already runs everything, you're only editing one part:
      the list of messages and small drawings it shows. this is done in
      an editor called Mu, which is already set up on the workshop
      laptops.
    </p>

    <h3>opening your code</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>plug your kit into the laptop with a USB-C cable, if it isn't already.</li>
      <li>your board should show up as a drive named <code>CIRCUITPY</code>, the same way a USB flash drive would.</li>
      <li>open <strong>Mu Editor</strong> on the laptop. it should detect your board automatically and show a message at the bottom of the window.</li>
      <li>in Mu, use <strong>File → Open</strong> (or the Load button) and choose <code>code.py</code> from the CIRCUITPY drive.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>editing the messages</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>scroll to the section near the top marked <code># MESSAGES + ASCII ART</code>. everything inside the square brackets below that comment is the part you can edit.</li>
      <li>each message is its own line, in quotes, followed by a comma. add a new one by copying that pattern, or remove one by deleting its line.</li>
      <li>keep messages short, the screen is small, and long lines wrap automatically onto a couple of lines if needed.</li>
      <li>you can also add small ASCII drawings the same way the "tiny sun" one is written, a few short lines of characters.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>saving and running it</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>save the file with <code>Cmd+S</code> (Mac) or <code>Ctrl+S</code> (Windows).</li>
      <li>the board notices the change and restarts on its own, this is normal, you'll see it happen in Mu's serial console at the bottom of the window.</li>
      <li>once it settles back on the intro screen, press the button once to start it running with your new messages.</li>
    </ol>

    <img
      class="step-image"
      src="/images/mu-editor-open.png"
      alt="Mu Editor open with code.py loaded, showing the file's imports and the start of the content list of messages and ASCII art."
    >
    <p class="note">
      want to see the full code with syntax and comments explained? the
      <a href="/code">code page</a> has the whole file, with a copy
      button if you want to grab it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="divider">✿</div>

  <section class="block">
    <span class="tag">part three</span>
    <h2>fabrication: build and decorate your tiny soft computer</h2>
    <p>
      this is the open-ended part, and the one with no fixed steps. see
      <a href="/fabrication">fabrication</a> for materials and a starting
      technique, but the form itself is yours to decide.
    </p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>soft, hard, exposed, hidden, anything goes.</li>
      <li>the only real constraints: it fits around your electronics, the screen stays visible, and the button stays reachable.</li>
      <li>we provide soft materials (felt, fabric, cardboard), and you're welcome to bring your own repurposed items too.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      your kit actually arrived in its own small cardboard box, and that
      box works perfectly well as a base prototype enclosure just as it
      is. cut a window for the screen, poke a hole for the button, and
      you're done, no extra materials required. but it's just a starting
      point, not a requirement. fabric, paper, another kind of box
      entirely, whatever material speaks to you is fair game. this part
      is meant to be explored, take your time and have fun with it.
    </p>

    <div class="placeholder-grid">
      <div class="diagram-placeholder">
        <p>cardboard</p>
        <p class="note" style="margin: 0;">
          the kit's own box, reused as-is
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="diagram-placeholder">
        <p>fabric</p>
        <p class="note" style="margin: 0;">
          a soft, sewn enclosure
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="diagram-placeholder">
        <p>paper</p>
        <p class="note" style="margin: 0;">
          folded, layered, or collaged
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <img
      class="step-image"
      src="/images/cardboard-enclosure.png"
      alt="The kit's cardboard box, cut and folded into a small enclosure with a window for the display, held in someone's hand."
    >
    <p class="note" style="margin-top: -0.6rem;">
      the kit's own box, cut and folded into a simple enclosure. see
      <a href="/fabrication">fabrication</a> for more starting points.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="divider">✿</div>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>a quick note on patience</h2>
    <p>
      the display can only redraw itself once every 180 seconds
      (3 minutes), that's the manufacturer's own spec for this exact
      panel, not something broken. press the button once, then let it
      be. more on why in <a href="/about">about</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

</Layout>